SECTION 9 — SECURITY REQUIREMENTS

Implement all relevant protections.

Database security

* Use PDO prepared statements.
* Never concatenate untrusted values into SQL.
* Validate sorting, filtering, and pagination parameters.
* Use database transactions for critical operations.

Cross-site scripting

* Escape untrusted output when rendering HTML.
* Validate and sanitize inputs according to their intended use.
* Do not treat input sanitization as a substitute for contextual output escaping.

CSRF

* Implement CSRF protection for state-changing requests.
* Validate tokens server-side.
* Use POST or another appropriate non-GET method for changes.

Authentication and authorization

* Hash passwords securely.
* Protect admin routes and actions.
* Regenerate sessions after authentication.
* Rate-limit login attempts.
* Use secure logout.
* Check permissions on the server for every protected action.

Sessions and cookies

* Use HttpOnly cookies.
* Enable Secure cookies when HTTPS is used.
* Use appropriate SameSite settings.
* Apply suitable session timeouts and session ID regeneration.

File uploads

* Validate actual file type and file size.
* Generate safe filenames.
* Prevent script execution in upload directories.
* Reject unsafe uploads.
* Do not trust client-supplied file extensions.

Business logic

* Recalculate product prices, stock, delivery charges, and totals on the server.
* Do not trust client-submitted order totals.
* Prevent negative quantities and negative stock.
* Protect against duplicate submissions.
* Validate order status transitions.
* Ensure cancellation and stock restoration cannot be repeated to manipulate inventory.

Error handling

* Do not display database credentials, stack traces, or sensitive server details to customers.
* Log appropriate errors securely.
* Display understandable error messages.
* Do not silently swallow critical failures.

